   "source": [
    "## Exercises:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "96a3ad95-3016-4728-8461-b3a6588ad86c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "1. Consider the following 2 quantites:\\\n",
    "   a. $(y_{n+1}|x=80) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 (80) + \\epsilon_{n+1}$ \\ (pred.)\n",
    "   and \\\n",
    "   b. $g(x = 80) = E(y_{n+1}|x = 80) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 (80)$. (conf)\n",
    "\n",
    "   What do they represent in the real life context of the problem?  Which is deterministic? Which is random? "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f803d1d9-1a48-43ce-9ba9-98347aa8f700",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "2. These quantities in a. and b. are our targets. The first is our target when we construct a prediction interval, the second is our target for a confidence interval. The point estimator for each is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\hat{y}_{(x = 80)} = \\hat{\\beta}_0 + \\hat{\\beta}_1 (80)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We will now try to understand why a PI is calculated differently than a CI. \n",
    "\n",
    "To do this, calculate the standard error of the difference between the point estimator and the target. This is basically like the expected error.\n",
    "\n",
    "In other words, show mathematically that:\\\n",
    "a. $SE(\\hat{y}_{(x = 80)} - (y_{n+1}|x=80)) = \\sigma \\sqrt{(1 +  \\frac{1}{n} + \\frac{(x - \\bar{x})^2  }{SSX})}$ and\\\n",
    "b. $SE(\\hat{y}_{(x = 80)} - g(x = 80)) = \\sigma \\sqrt{( \\frac{1}{n} + \\frac{(x - \\bar{x})^2  }{SSX})}$\n",
    "\n",
    "(Hint: pay attention to the error term in part a.)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6727dd56-5cf2-4907-be56-6506687cf27b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "3.  For part a. (the prediction interval) adjust the margin of error to account for what you showed in part 2a. Calculate a new interval using the adjusted standard error. "
   ]
  },
